  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Patchy dense fog early Monday morning.\n\n-  Warm, dry week ahead for the region.\n\n-  Showers return late next week or early next weekend.\n\nHigh pressure took hold over the Northern Rockies today. Recent\nrainfall and stable conditions was a recipe for patchy dense fog\nthis morning, which will continue each morning for the next few\ndays.\n\nAnother low pressure system is moving into the West Coast and\nheading toward the Desert Southwest. There is also a broad trough\nestablished across the northern Pacific Ocean. These two features\nwill bring winds from the southwest into the Northern Rockies. As\na result, the region will see warm and mild conditions most of the\nweek. Afternoon temperatures will be about 10 to 12 degrees above\nnormal with light winds and partly cloudy skies.\n\nAs the broad trough over the northern Pacific moves eastward, it\nwill bring widespread rainfall and isolated thunderstorms back to\nthe Northern Rockies by the end of the coming week. Expect\nincreasing westerly winds as a cold front moves through the area.\nSnow levels will remain high this week, likely falling to 6500 to\n7000 feet behind the front next weekend.",
